File: RandomPoints.info, Node: dimViaBezout, Next: ExtendField, Prev: DimensionFunction, Up: Top

dimViaBezout -- computes the dimension of the given ideal $I$ probabilistically
*******************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        dimViaBezout(I)
  * Inputs:
      * I, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, in a polynomial ring over a
        field
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * DimensionIntersectionAttempts => an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,,
        default value null, the number of linear spaces to try before moving to
        the next dimension
      * MinimumFieldSize => an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, default value
        null, if the ambient field is smaller than this value it will
        automatically be replaced with an extension
      * Homogeneous => ..., default value true
      * *note Replacement: Replacement, => ..., default value Full, When
        changing coordinates, whether to replace variables by general degree 1
        forms, binomials, etc.
      * Verbose => ..., default value false
  * Outputs:
      * an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, d = dimension of the ideal $I$

Description
===========

This intersects $V(I)$ with successively higher dimensional random linear
spaces until there is an intersection.  For example, if $V(I)$ intersect a
random line has a point, then we expect that $V(I)$ contains a hypersurface.
If there was no intersection, this function tries a 2-dimensional linear space,
and so on.  This greatly speeds up some computations, although in other
examples, the built in dim function is much faster.

+---------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : kk=ZZ/101;                                                |
+---------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : S=kk[y_0..y_8];                                           |
+---------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : I=ideal random(S^1,S^{-2,-2,-2,-2})+(ideal random(2,S))^2;|
|                                                               |
|o3 : Ideal of S                                                |
+---------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : elapsedTime dimViaBezout(I)                               |
| -- 1.90125s elapsed                                           |
|                                                               |
|o4 = 4                                                         |
+---------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : elapsedTime dim I                                         |
| -- 4.05805s elapsed                                           |
|                                                               |
|o5 = 4                                                         |
+---------------------------------------------------------------+

The user may set the MinimumFieldSize to ensure that the field being worked
over is big enough.  For instance, there are relatively few linear spaces over
a field of characteristic 2, and this can cause incorrect results to be
provided.  If no size is provided, the function tries to guess a good size
based on ambient ring.

If the option Homogeneous=>true then we use homogeneous linear spaces if the
ideal itself is homogeneous.  Otherwise our linear spaces are not homogeneous.

The user may also specify what sort of linear forms to intersect with via the
*note Replacement: Replacement, option.

See also
========

  * *note DimensionFunction: DimensionFunction, -- an option for specifying
    custom dimension functions

Ways to use dimViaBezout:
=========================

  * "dimViaBezout(Ideal)"

